{"kind":"report","text":"Proceed with one bounded P1f gate-and-selection package. There is no newly identified cryptographic feasibility blocker. The remaining P1 requirements are an executable real gate, its final source-bound readiness evidence, and the actual conditional independent authority chain. This report is advice under request #1077, not selection, gate passage, implementation permission or deployment approval.\n\nI inspected landed main 1c158d8044cd53ceed5cd191f1f423613c3c571e, tree 3b08657ab32e7dd43a04c1b2fa1ae7eb1f995f44; request #1077 and publication checkpoint #1076; current gate/decision/evidence contracts, Plan 001 and ratified assessments #870/#871. I reuse review #997: 119 runtime cases, 10 authority groups with all 71 required subchecks, 43 independent CLI challenges and a cache-free installation/check/build. I did not rerun those suites or edit source. GitSeq primary rules were checked at clean d630554a7ba7d99c5f95eb77e1dfa7fd1f41637a, not attributed to the older installed bin/gs.\n\n1. Exact remaining work and scope.\n\nAt 1c158d8, scripts/protocol-gates.ts always throws after checking optional evidence; this is an intentional unimplemented activation boundary, not a discovered crypto defect. scripts/decision-validator.ts:26–30 permits only the existing real diagnostic contract, :93–106 requires live exact source artifacts and guarded bindings, and :119–123 returns a diagnostic. scripts/decision-evidence.ts requires the actual baseline commit as well as tree to equal manifest S, and an exhaustive 129 case IDs (119 runtime plus 10 authority groups), with raw 71-subcheck verification. Do not replace any of these checks with a boolean or report ID.\n\nCommission a small gate wrapper plus a development readiness runner. Proposed new paths: scripts/protocol-gate-core.ts, scripts/protocol-gate-readiness.ts, scripts/protocol-gate-readiness-evidence.ts, fixtures/crypto/gates/profile.json, tests/protocol-gates/{authority.test.ts,cli.test.ts}, and a dedicated protocol-gates.config.ts/tsconfig.protocol-gates.json if needed. Focused edits: scripts/protocol-gates.ts, decision-types.ts, decision-validator.ts, decision-evidence.ts, package.json, docs/protocol-adoption.md and docs/toolchain.md. Permit only the necessary evidence plumbing in scripts/protocol-closure.ts, protocol-closure-evidence.ts, decision-public-evidence.ts, fixtures/crypto/closure/profile.json, tests/protocol-closure/authority.test.ts and its fixture/challenge helpers. Enumerate the actual final changed paths rather than adopting this prospective list as an artifact map.\n\nKeep provider pins, cipher/profile identities, lower MLS algorithms, recovery/custody, gateway semantics, existing cases and all resource ceilings unchanged. No new production services, dependencies, signer capability, crypto design, P2–P9 implementation or deployment. Preserve the cache-free generic check; dedicated crypto/gate typechecks run after their required preparation in the full runner.\n\n2. One selection, after publication; no source/evidence cycle.\n\nUse this concrete sequence:\n\n(a) Builder implements every gate, validator, evidence checker and new test before freezing the publication candidate C. An independent code/publication review covers all changed artifacts and the full development evidence at C. This review does not approve a protocol selection. Builder requester-ratifies it and performs ordinary guarded publication.\n\n(b) Complete publication and freeze exact landed S, with a fixed source base and exhaustive diff(base,S). Inspect the real receipt and all live builder-authored merge-successor artifacts A at S. Run the full readiness package at that exact clean S to produce final E. Keep the earlier candidate E unchanged as historical publication evidence. Final E contains S, profiles and raw observations, but no future D, AD, Q, P, R, AR, operational instruction or gate-success output.\n\nThis is intentionally a final source-bound run at the landed commit: current evidence validation compares commit identity, so even an identical-tree merge does not permit changing C to S in earlier JSON. A candidate E remains valid historical proof of C at its recorded frontier; it is never promised as the final selection E. Selecting only after publication prevents that publication from retiring the selected artifacts. This costs a final run at S, avoids a new receipt-alias validation scheme, and does not waive a source-world check.\n\n(c) Builder proposes D, directly resting on EVERY selected A, with the strict manifest binding exact S/base/tree, every changed path/blob/SHA-256/event, the selected ordering@1/recovery@2 profile, final E and its complete raw closure, required cases/subchecks and verbatim D1–D9 choices. Root explicitly ratifies that proposal as AD. Both state that the selection is conditional on independent approval and every predicate, and authorize no operational gate run alone. Do not use the alternative authority-bearing-request adoption path.\n\n(d) Builder requests Q only after AD, resting on D, AD and every A, addressed to the independent reviewer, with no_git_artifact=true and the exact manifest/E fields. Reviewer promises P, independently checks S/E/D/AD and all A, and files an actual guarded exact-head R with strict decision-approval JSON. Builder, as Q requester, ratifies R as AR. Root cannot substitute for that requester ratification. The order is S/A/E -\u003e D -\u003e AD -\u003e Q -\u003e P -\u003e R -\u003e AR, with explicit semantic act order independent of JSON property order.\n\nUse the existing guard's decision-adoption mode for this already-published artifact set: gs review --self-initiated D, only after verifying that the primary is not any commitment's report and has no direct author-owned request/promise edge, and that no companion reports an assignment. This is a new independent decision review of existing published artifacts, not relabelling the old assigned candidate or its publication verdict. No second merge follows this decision-only review.\n\nThe support is concrete: GitSeq internal/reviewguard/binding.go:402–429 explicitly accepts D directly adopting A and rejects disguised assignments. Current Noseq decision-validator.ts:104 already accepts that actual guarded binding. GitSeq fold.go:2554 and receipt_checkpoint_test.go preserve the genuine receipt-to-successor checkpoint while retaining source-world and later-condemnation failures. Current A1001/13093a7a and A1002/aff40ea2 exemplify builder-authored live artifacts resting only on the actual publication receipt; they are not the old reporting candidates. Apply the same inspected shape to P1f, without fabricating a receipt or filing replacement A against a completed assignment.\n\nIf the actual publication graph does not meet these predicates, stop before D and resolve that graph explicitly. Do not hide a direct retired-artifact edge behind a new label. Subsequent retirement/condemnation of selected A or authority acts must still invalidate the chain at a frontier containing it. A later merge or changed gate source does not inherit this selection; it needs fresh exact bindings. No source commit contains a future act ID or an excluded hash region.\n\n3. Executable commands, trusted inputs and actual operation.\n\nAdd npm run probe:protocol-gate-readiness and verify:protocol-gate-readiness as development commands that never open a real gate. Final E recursively verifies all 119 runtime cases, all 71 existing authority subchecks and a fixed manifest of the narrowly added gate cases. Distinguish counts: the ten authority groups are not ten runtime crypto cases. Preserve all previous negative cases, including rejected Marmot and the sorted/reversed-key positives.\n\nUse a new readiness evidence version rather than asserting global non-adoption forever. Existing protocol-closure.ts:38 embeds “decisions-unadopted”; authority.test.ts:129–145 selects a moving real head but deliberately supplies chain:null. For repeatable development, retain that negative against a named immutable real public pre-selection frontier F_pre and state exactly what is absent in that fixture. New readiness conclusions mean “observations checked; no operational gate result in this record.” They remain true after a later real selection. Do not delete a negative or turn the development runner into a command that expects real adoption to remain absent at the current room head.\n\nPublic commands:\nnpm run test:crypto-feasibility -- --decision-export EXPORT --trust-policy POLICY --gitseq PINNED_BINARY --output NEW_DIRECTORY\nnpm run gate:protocol -- --decision-export EXPORT --trust-policy POLICY --gitseq PINNED_BINARY --output NEW_DIRECTORY\n\nBoth verify the already selected immutable E and genuine signed authority; they do not create a different E on every invocation. E-only positional input, copied diagnostic results, synthetic policies, unknown arguments and all override/adopted/test switches fail. Success explicitly says “G1–G5 bounded P1 gates passed for S/profile/E at F”, identifies D/AD/Q/P/R/AR and the operational commission, and makes no production or global-freshness claim. A result JSON is a diagnostic observation, never a bearer approval; each invocation re-verifies inputs.\n\nUse an externally supplied, operator-trusted gate policy wrapping the strict decision expectations plus an exact operational commission ID. Pin genesis, independent root/reviewer/implementer fingerprints, S/base/tree, exhaustive paths, profile/dependencies/runtimes, E closure and case/subcheck contracts, D1–D9, and minimum F. Keep the pinned independently built d630 verifier and its provenance/digest; neither the bundle nor an executable path chosen by it supplies trust. Arbitrarily placing a policy outside a bundle does not make it trusted: root/operator deliberately selects and controls this configuration. The public entry point accepts only the real-room mode and real principal policy. Generated-room positives remain restricted to the separate development diagnostic API.\n\nThe executing gate/checker must come from the separately trusted clean checkout of exact S, with the loaded module root, source identity and runtime/dependency pins checked. Imported source objects remain data and are not executed. A valid bundle for S must not bless a changed currently executing gate. No mutation of the shared resident, native relay installation or live-key custody is needed.\n\nAfter AR, root issues one ordinary operational request C_op to the intended operator/builder, no_git_artifact=true, resting on the exact chain and naming S, manifest/E hashes and the two permitted commands. It is the express operational commission required by #871, not another proposal or another ratification of D. The gate checks its real root signer, effective/unretired status and exact scope/bindings at F; F includes AR and C_op, and the trusted policy pins C_op. D, AD, R or AR alone cannot authorize command success.\n\nRoot initially sets the external minimum to a trusted signed frontier including C_op. Maintain an operator-controlled nondecreasing minimum/high-water mark outside exports, with atomic advancement and refusal of an older or sibling offered frontier. Never advance it from unverified claimed depth. Once a complete signed descendant prefix is verified, retain its high-water mark even if the selected authority is then rejected because that prefix reveals a revocation; a failed gate must not allow a subsequent older bundle to erase known revocation. Retained old valid F does not prove absence of unseen revocation; rolled-back trusted custody cannot magically detect its own rollback. If currentness beyond the trusted minimum is required, acquire a newer independently trusted frontier or refuse. This limitation matches the accepted named-prefix model rather than implying global freshness.\n\nOperator promises C_op, runs both commands, and retains raw stdout/stderr, exit codes, command/source/policy/binary/export digests and results as V. Report V under that ordinary request for root inspection/ratification. Root may independently repeat the inexpensive detached gate verification without rerunning 119 runtime cases. V references E and the authority chain; E never references V. R approves the implemented predicates and evidence before V exists. No second selection ceremony or post-review code patch is necessary.\n\n4. Discriminating acceptance checks.\n\nAdd a bounded, strict required-case set covering:\n- Real public CLI refuses an otherwise valid synthetic decision, a copied result, missing commission, wrong/retired/non-root commission, wrong command/scope, and partial D/AD/Q/P/R/AR. Retain existing independent-actor, ratification replacement, wrong-order, retirement, conflicting decision, signature, complete-prefix, resource and raw-evidence negatives.\n- Actual signed generated-room guard/fold exercise of publication -\u003e live successor A -\u003e D/AD -\u003e fresh decision review, with the old candidate retired. Test direct retired-artifact/world contamination, later successor condemnation and attempts to reuse a publication-only review. Preserve exact source distinction: same tree/different commit does not rewrite E, and a different-tree publication fails the equality/selection expectation. Test native guard/fold publication separately from crypto E when necessary; do not build a synthetic E containing its own review.\n- Internally valid generated authority plus archived accepted E can exercise diagnostic positive predicates, as P1e already does; it must still fail the real public entry point. Only the later genuine real-room V combines final S/E with real principals and actual approval.\n- Changed loaded gate source, dirty checkout, wrong runtime/verifier/profile, omitted gate case, omitted one of the preserved 71 subchecks, duplicate/failed/rehashed reports, and a changed raw byte fail. No weakening of existing byte/object/file/time/depth bounds.\n- A pinned F_pre missing-selection negative remains repeatable after an isolated fixture advances through a later selection. Trusted minimum/high-water refuses rollback/sibling, including a valid-old retry after a newer verified prefix caused revocation failure; valid-at-F diagnostics do not claim unseen freshness.\n- Actual cache-free npm ci/check/build still passes, dedicated gate typecheck is in the prepared full runner, and all pre-existing future-stage commands still fail.\n\nRetain failed attempts and all CLI outcomes, source/build provenance and exact inventories. At candidate C and final S, the full readiness runner is necessary to bind the changed source; this is not a request to repeat accepted P1e now. Before D, actual real entry points must fail. After AR/C_op, actual success of BOTH commands plus a targeted invalid-input refusal is the final operational acceptance observation V. If any missing positive branch requires further source changes, return to a new source/evidence/review cycle, not a test flag.\n\n5. G1–G5 disposition and D1–D9 limits.\n\nG1: accepted immutable-order authenticated MLS, provisional winner/loser handling, removal with actual terminal/lower-API failure and non-removal catch-up control, real membership churn and Node/Chromium cases. D1 selects Noseq rather than the rejected Marmot pair; D6 retains online owner and common-last-good failure behavior; D9 keeps exact lower-API/runtime pins. No additional Marmot convergence experiment is required to select its explicitly incompatible alternative.\n\nG2: accepted segmented genesis-to-eligible-T history, per-message openings, owner-attested control intervals, complete retained-identity readback, uncertainty/omission/refusal, bounded assembly and compact owner/sealer/client reconstruction. D2 limits the promise to eligible retained closure; D3 is owner attestation, not independent replay of encrypted historical commits; D4 retains old opening material and weaker history forward secrecy; D5 retains immutable-owner/old-backup continuation authority. Joint byte/identity/segment ceilings are not an unlimited-history or full-size 65,536-event promise. P1 evidence includes the 10,000-small-action composition vector, not a production 10,000-entry replay benchmark.\n\nG3: accepted admission/owner context binding, ordering/retries/removal and staged pending interleaving. D5/D6 require owner availability and fail-closed handling of invalid encrypted content; D8 preserves the tested signer capability boundary. P8 external onboarding is not an unclosed P1 test.\n\nG4: accepted canonical original proofs, encrypted/ordered wire binding, pins, limits and actual local browser/Node/Workers/native behavior. D1/D9 choose the experimental Noseq profile; hosted interoperability, production transaction/outbox crashes and throughput remain later work, not omitted claims.\n\nG5: accepted actual isolated private gateway/strfry sockets, restricted request surfaces, stopped replication recovery, highest-prefix ACL, learned-control dequeue/send fence, restore fencing, closure holes and conservative quota reservations. D7 explicitly accepts unseen-removal old-history exposure while forbidding known-revocation downgrade; D8 exposes membership/identity/existence/timing/size metadata to the specified operators; D9 distinguishes local isolation from deployment guarantees. Bare public strfry is not substituted for the gateway.\n\nThus the missing P1 requirement is the final gate/authority composition above, plus real conditional acceptance of D1–D9. If root rejects any of these limits, STOP selection and commission the specific policy redesign; do not mark a gate passed through a vague “pending” qualification. If the package or its exact-head review fails, CHANGE that package. If all predicates and real V succeed, PROCEED only with bounded P1 closure and a separately commissioned next stage.\n\n6. Minimal Plan reconciliation.\n\nBefore P2, root coordinates a narrow update of Plan 001's P1/P2 selected-profile wording and current evidence/status pointers. Its P1 G1 instructions still describe Marmot branch/witness/withdrawal behavior, and P2.1 currently requires compliant Marmot application content with its inner-signature prohibition. Replace these with the actually adopted Noseq immutable-order signed-proof/MLS application contract and explicit retained Marmot rejection; keep D1–D9 and the exact S/E/authority/V references. Do not imply interoperability or delete the STOP vector.\n\nPreserve the completed Atseq revision and its full source closure, custody/reconstruction/provenance limits; retain P5 retained-bytes proof, P6 decrypted recovery, P7 direct JSON/Float32Array UI, P8 onboarding and P9 acceptance/benchmarks. A later planning document does not alter the selected executable S. Continue to invoke the pinned S gate; do not claim that a newer main commit was itself selected merely because it contains those documents. Any changed gate/validator source requires fresh bindings. This report neither edits the Atseq-owned documents nor authorizes P2.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","recommendation":"proceed with bounded P1f package; publish closed implementation first, then bind final E and real conditional authority to landed S","scope":"read-only P1f gate-and-conditional-selection advice; no adoption or gate passage","source":"1c158d8044cd53ceed5cd191f1f423613c3c571e"}}